<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.5.3: Distance as a Quadratic Function of Elapsed Time</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01816</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.5.3: Distance as a Quadratic Function of Elapsed Time</md:title>
    <md:uuid>1997b3f2-f8c0-406d-982f-01cf0c361c36</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Galileo Galilei, an Italian scientist, and other medieval scholars studied the motion of free-falling objects. The
  law they discovered can be expressed by the equation \(d=16t^2\), which gives the distance fallen in feet as a
  function of time, \(t\), in seconds.</para>
<para>An object is dropped from a height of 576 feet.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><title>Activity</title><exercise data-content-id="482c9c67-5fb3-42e8-9dfa-474eb2895614" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here:</para><para>How many feet does it fall in 0.5 seconds?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! It falls 4 feet in 0.5 seconds because \(16 (0.5)^2=4\).</para><para>The correct answer is 4. It falls 4 feet in 0.5 seconds because \(16 (0.5)^2=4\).</para></solution></exercise></section><list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>To find out where the object is the first few seconds after it was dropped, Elena and Diego created different
    tables.</item>
</list>
<para>Elena’s table:</para>
<table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>
        Time (Seconds)
      </emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>
        Distance Fallen (Feet)
      </emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        0
      </entry>
      <entry>
        0
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        1
      </entry>
      <entry>
        16
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        2
      </entry>
      <entry>
        64
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        3
      </entry>
      <entry/>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        4
      </entry>
      <entry/>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(t\)
      </entry>
      <entry/>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<para>Diego’s table:</para>
<table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>
        Time (Seconds)
      </emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>
        Distance from the Ground (Feet)
      </emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        0
      </entry>
      <entry>
        576
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        1
      </entry>
      <entry>
        560
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        2
      </entry>
      <entry>
        512
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        3
      </entry>
      <entry/>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        4
      </entry>
      <entry/>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        t
      </entry>
      <entry/>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> How are the two tables alike?</para>
      </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>They both have time in seconds and distance in feet as the two quantities being measured. They both show
      0–4 seconds and \(t\) seconds for time. They describe the same object falling.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> How are the two tables different?</para>
      </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Elena’s table measures distance fallen, and Diego’s table measures distance from the ground. They
      record measurements from opposite ends. Elena’s table starts with 0 feet for 0 seconds, and Diego’s
      starts with 576 feet for 0 seconds.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Complete Elena’s table. Be prepared to explain your reasoning.</item>
</list>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="07cfb666-b9a1-4cc5-b6a4-78e440bd664e" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here:</para><para> What is the distance the object has fallen 3 seconds after it was dropped?</para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! 144. The object has fallen 144 feet 3 seconds after it was dropped.</para><para>The correct answer is 144. The object has fallen 144 feet 3 seconds after it was dropped.</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="9cb0e6c1-2f5c-4b7f-b400-addd69c11b52" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here:</para><para> What is the distance the object has fallen 4 seconds after it was dropped?</para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! 256. The object has fallen 256 feet 4 seconds after it was dropped.</para><para>The correct answer is 256. The object has fallen 256 feet 4 seconds after it was dropped.</para></solution></exercise></section>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> What is the distance the object has fallen \(t\) seconds after it was dropped? </para>
      <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\(16t^2\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Complete Diego’s table. Be prepared to explain your reasoning.</item>
</list>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="f680c9cc-d48d-4627-921e-1837b60ec9f1" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here:</para><para> What is the object’s distance from the ground 3 seconds after it was dropped?</para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! 432. The object is 432 feet from the ground 3 seconds after it was dropped.</para><para>The correct answer is 432. The object is 432 feet from the ground 3 seconds after it was dropped.</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="fea13522-15ea-4820-b214-42f58d143dad" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here:</para><para> What is the object’s distance from the ground 4 seconds after it was dropped?</para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! 320. The object is 320 feet from the ground 4 seconds after it was dropped.</para><para>The correct answer is 320. The object is 320 feet from the ground 4 seconds after it was dropped.</para></solution></exercise></section>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> What is the object’s distance from the ground \(t\) seconds after it was dropped? </para>
      <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your Answer:</para>
  <para>\(576-16t^2\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>

